No words left behind
====================

Stopping dictation lost the tail of the sentence, and it was structural rather than a bug in the
loop. The talk service ends an utterance after `CLOSE_MS` of silence — 2.2 seconds, raised from 700 ms
because a thinking pause is not the end of a sentence. Click stop while still talking and those words
sit in a segment that never closes, so they are never transcribed. The last few seconds of every
recording went missing, which is why it looked like it "skipped the last five seconds" and why it
"only worked when it had caught up".

Two things fix it, and both are needed:

* the service grew a `POST /flush`, which closes the open utterance on demand instead of waiting for
  silence, and publishes the in-progress transcript as `partial` in `/state`. The engine calls flush
  before it releases the microphone, so the sentence being spoken when the button is pressed is part
  of the transcript;
* the composer inserts the `partial` as soon as stop is pressed, then replaces that exact range with
  the final read when it lands — and only if the box still contains exactly what was written there,
  so somebody typing over it keeps their own words. Waiting for a full re-transcription of audio that
  was already transcribed is what "I shouldn't have to wait a second or two" was about.

Measured in a container on an abrupt, no-trailing-silence clip: text in the box **0.6 s** after the
press, final text identical, no duplication.

